The single weakest element is the provenance paragraph itself, in the Transaction pane's `DATA` row. It is set **right-aligned** — nine lines of body prose with a ragged left edge, from `Every block, transaction, address and` down to `published under.` It inherited the alignment of the value column it was dropped into, which is correct for `Yes`, `Safe` and a hash and wrong for a paragraph, and it is the only paragraph on the page set this way: the two other explanations in the same pane, under EXECUTIONS and DECODED INPUT, are left-aligned and full-width. It also sits inside the 275 px value column, so the label column's 90 px runs empty beside all nine lines.

The band was removed so this claim would stop interrupting and start reading. It now reads worse than the copy around it, in the one row where the reader is meant to learn whose data this is. Left-align it and let it span the pane.

